WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.600
In a fundamental shift, React Native 0.76 completely replaces the application's legacy communication bridge with a direct memory sharing architecture.

2
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:19.200
For years, JavaScript and Native Code operated on completely isolated execution threads. They shared no physical memory.

3
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:26.400
Every time they communicated, they translated data into JSON, used an asynchronous queue, and waited for parsing.

4
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:38.400
This translation created severe latency. A single round trip took tens of milliseconds, expanding well past the 16.6 millisecond threshold required for 60 frames per second.

5
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:49.400
When an application attempted to execute high frequency operations, like tracking a user's finger during a swipe gesture, or reading a hardware gyroscope, that asynchronous queue backed up.

6
00:00:49.400 --> 00:01:04.400
The interface would skip frames and visibly stutter. A system built on copying serialized text across an asynchronous gap was incapable of meeting modern rendering demands. The entire architecture had to shift from copying data to sharing it.

7
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:20.400
The solution is the JavaScript interface, or JSI. It is a synchronous C++ communication layer that completely eliminates the JSON queue. Under JSI, JavaScript and Native Code now run inside the exact same process space.

8
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.400
Sharing a process means sharing memory. JavaScript no longer waits for copies. It points directly to existing native data.

9
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:35.400
A pointer referencing a C++ buffer passes to the JavaScript thread, instantly treated as an array buffer. Zero bytes are copied.

10
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:52.400
By removing the queue and the serialization steps, round-trip communication latency drops from tens of milliseconds to mere microseconds. Operating at a microsecond scale is what finally allows React Native to execute frame-by-frame animations and complex gestures at a flawless 60 frames per second.

11
00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:04.400
To understand how this shared memory works, we have to look at the C++ bedrock of JSI. It begins with the JSI.Runtime, the actual execution environment provided by the React Native host.

12
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:13.400
This runtime is strictly threadifying. Attempting to store a reference to it and execute it on a background thread will trigger undefined behavior and crash your application.

13
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:23.400
Within this runtime, every piece of JavaScript data from a simple boolean to a complex object is represented by a single C++ type called JSI.Value.

14
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:38.400
JSI.Value uses C++ move semantics. To duplicate data, you must explicitly call .as object. This manual duplication is required because the Hermes-Garmt-Grabot-Grabot-Grabot-Grabots act of references. When that counter takes down to zero, the engine instantly clears the memory.

15
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Working with JSI means native developers can no longer rely on automatic bridge translations. You are now directly responsible for managing the lifecycle and memory references of your JavaScript data.

16
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:58.400
So how do we actually expose complex iOS features like the camera or the file system to the C++ runtime? JSI provides a structural pattern for this called JSI.HOST object.

17
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:13.400
It is a C++ interface specifically designed to hold stateful native objects. During initialization, you implement your custom logic inside a C++ class and inject that host object directly into the JavaScript global scope.

18
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:23.400
Once installed, execution is immediate. A function call in JavaScript bypasses all intermediate layers, triggering the C++ logic synchronously and instantly.

19
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:34.400
And because they share the same memory space, the Hermes engine automatically maintains the C++ object via shared pointers, keeping it alive for exactly as long as the JavaScript side holds an active reference.

20
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:50.400
The host object pattern achieves true zero latency communication, but it permanently intertwines the life cycles of your native instances and your JavaScript objects. This introduces a major friction point for modern iOS developers. Why can't we just write these host objects directly in Swift?

21
00:03:51.400 --> 00:04:07.400
Swift has evolving C++ interoperability, but hits a hard roadblock against the complex architecture of JSI. JSI.HOST object uses virtual inheritance and templates, which Swift cannot parse.

22
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:25.400
To bridge this gap, we must use Objective C++, the .MM format, as a translation layer. The client compiler treats .MM files as standard Objective C, but grants them unrestricted native access to the complete C++ API.

23
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:36.400
This requires a specific three-file architecture for any iOS turbo module. First, an Objective C header file exposes only clean, safe Objective C types.

24
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:55.400
Second, the .MM implementation file encapsulates all of the C++ complexity. This is where you instantiate the host object and execute the raw JSI logic. Finally, your Swift code accesses that clean Objective C interface through a standard bridging header. Swift never sees the C++ types.

25
00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:06.400
Objective C++ is often viewed as a relic, but in modern react native iOS development, it is the essential encapsulation mechanism required to achieve maximum performance.

26
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:22.400
This comparative data table shows exactly what that performance looks like. By comparing the legacy bridge to JSI, we see round-trip latency drop from tens of milliseconds down to microseconds alongside the new ability to access memory directly via pointers.

27
00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:37.400
These microseconds speeds make high-intensity tasks viable, such as real-time image processing, direct sensor reads, and Apple Metal API integration. However, the synchronous nature of JSI creates a significant operational risk.

28
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:50.400
Under the legacy bridge, native operations ran in the background, but in JSI, a slow calculation on the native C++ side will instantly paralyze the JavaScript thread, freezing the entire application interface.

29
00:05:50.400 --> 00:06:03.400
To avoid this synchronous trap, developers must strictly limit synchronous JSI calls to operations that execute in microseconds, such as reading state from memory or initializing callbacks.

30
00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:14.400
Any computationally heavy task must be explicitly offloaded to a background native thread using standard JavaScript promises to return the data once the work is complete.

31
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:32.400
React Native 0.76 grants iOS developers micro-second-level execution by completely removing the communication bottleneck, but wielding this shared memory architecture safely requires strict discipline over C++ constraints and low-level memory management.

